# THE WHOLE CLIENT COST OF THIS TOPOLOGY.
#
# Two stanzas. Neither names a host, so adding a target to the fleet changes
# nothing here — which is the property that makes an evaluation across twelve
# hosts a config change on one box rather than twelve installs.

# The host NO target covers. The bastion carries this forward blind — it
# relays bytes it cannot read — so the certificate verified at the far end is
# the client's own, and this host has to trust the CA itself.
#
# It must come FIRST, and that ordering is the whole difference between the
# two modes expressed in client config. ssh takes the first value it finds
# for each keyword, so a blind destination listed in the stanza below would
# inherit HostKeyAlias hoop-bastion — and then store blind-01's OWN host key
# under the bastion's name, which collides with the bastion's key the moment
# any terminated host is used. The symptom is REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS
# CHANGED on a stack where nothing changed.
#
# So: HostKeyAlias is for destinations the bastion TERMINATES, because those
# are the ones where the bastion's key answers in the target's place.
Host blind-01
    ProxyJump jump@172.31.78.10:2222
    User devuser

Host *.prod legacy-01
    # ProxyJump is the mechanism. It does not log in to the bastion and run
    # ssh there: it opens a forwarded channel and speaks SSH over it, and the
    # literal string you typed — "host-a.prod" — travels in that channel-open
    # request. That is the one place in the SSH protocol where an unmodified
    # client tells an intermediary the exact host it wants. There is no SNI.
    #
    # `jump@` is a NAME, not an account. The bastion has no accounts at all;
    # it checks the name against the certificate's principals and never looks
    # it up in a user database, because no session runs there. The account on
    # the TARGET is the User below — or a per-target `login` override.
    ProxyJump jump@172.31.78.10:2222

    # HostKeyAlias points every target's host-key lookup at ONE name, so the
    # bastion's own key answers for all of them and one known_hosts entry
    # covers the fleet.
    #
    # It is necessary rather than convenient: the bastion terminates hop 2,
    # so it presents ITS key where the target's would be, and without this
    # every connection is a host-key mismatch. The cost is real and named in
    # the ADR — a user who bypasses the bastion gets a first-contact prompt
    # rather than a mismatch warning, so network policy has to carry what
    # host-key checking no longer does.
    HostKeyAlias hoop-bastion
    User devuser

# The one target that authenticates with the user's OWN certificate. One
# extra line, and it is the line that puts a person's name in that host's
# auth.log instead of an account's.
#
# ForwardAgent is offered on HOP 2, inside the terminated session, because
# ProxyJump opens no session channel on the bastion and the agent request is
# a session request. The bastion CONSUMES the agent to sign its upstream
# handshake and never forwards the channel onward to the target.
Host host-c.prod
    ForwardAgent yes

Host *
    StrictHostKeyChecking accept-new
    # /tmp, because ~/.ssh holds read-only bind mounts and docker creates
    # that directory root-owned: the client cannot write a known_hosts into
    # it, and ssh then warns on every single command.
    UserKnownHostsFile /tmp/known_hosts
    IdentitiesOnly yes
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
    CertificateFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519-cert.pub
    # INFO, not ERROR. A refused channel — a capability a target does not
    # admit, a forward outside the allowlist, a credential that did not
    # resolve — is reported by the client at this level and SWALLOWED at
    # ERROR. On a stack whose whole subject is refusals, hiding them makes
    # every denial look like a hang.
    LogLevel INFO
